'''Zocodover''' ({{Literal translation|market of burden beasts}}; {{Langx|mxi|ثُوكُو ذُوبِيرْ|Zokođóber}}) is an [[W:Artificial island|artificial island]] in [[Salto Bay]], within the [[Sástago]] borough of [[Castejón]]. It is one of the [[Salto Islands]].

The site of modern-day Zocodover was originally swamp land that in the 1880s was [[W:Land reclamation|reclaimed]] and gradually became a bustling dock area as part of the [[Port of Sástago]], with an extensive network of wharfs, heavy rail infrastructure and light industry. Following the [[W:Containerisation|containerisation]] of shipping traffic, the Port of Sástago fell into disuse as traffic shifted to the [[Port of Jatiel]], and by the 1990s it was virtually abandoned. The final nail in the coffin was a [[1992 Salto Bay referendum|1992 referendum]] which banned most activity from water-based industries in [[Salto Bay]]. 

The groundbreaking for the [[Castejón Skytree]] in 2009 spurred significant interest in undertaking [[W:Urban renewal|urban renewal]] on the island, and it has since experienced a boom in housing construction. Now known for its [[W:Contemporary architecture|contemporary architecture]], the island is home to a number of heritage buildings that have been retained for [[W:Adaptive reuse|adaptive reuse]].

